um_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Rule 6c-11(b) provides four categories of relief to ETFs that meet the rule's conditions:**\n\n1. **Redeemability:** ETF shares count as \"redeemable securities\" (Section 2(a)(32)), so ETFs can operate as open-end funds while redeeming only in creation units.\n2. **Secondary-market pricing:** Shares can trade at market prices rather than NAV, with relief from Sections 22(d) and 22c-1.\n3. **Affiliate in-kind transactions:** Relief from Sections 17(a)(1) and (2).\n4. **Delayed redemption delivery:** Limited relief from Section 22(e)'s seven-day requirement.\n\n**Affiliate limits:** Relief covers only in-kind creations and redemptions by persons who are affiliated *solely* because they own 5% or more (first-tier) or 25% or more of the ETF's shares, or by affiliates of such holders (second-tier). It does not cover affiliation through the adviser or sponsor.\n\n**Foreign-delivery limits:** Delivery may be postponed only for foreign investments when local holidays or settlement practices prevent timely delivery. Delivery must occur as soon as practicable, and no later than 15 days.","ttft_ms":6523,"type":"result","duration_ms":9872,"uuid":"9e98e4f4-f14c-41be-9d78-77e68df1b311","ttft_stream_ms":1169,"time_to_request_ms":52,"first_content_frame_ms":1169,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
